\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" 13194139535730 true] [17592186363788 1113 #uuid "02b892c4-044a-53a5-940f-9a5ebb949669" 13194139535730 true] [17592186363788 1145 "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535730 true] [17592186363789 1000 #edb/ref 1759218636